   100	**The signature is mandatory.** Exhaustiveness is only a well-posed question against a *declared*
   101	input type — a language that infers the function type from its own clauses can never ask it,
   102	because the answer is always yes. **shipped**
   103	
   104	**Guards** use `when`, with `and` and `or`. A guard the checker can read as a type operation
   105	refines the clause; one it cannot read credits nothing. **shipped**
   106	
   107	```csharp
   108	Classify(n) when n < 10              -> :low
   109	Classify(n) when n >= 10 and n < 100 -> :mid
   110	Classify(n) when n >= 100            -> :high
   111	```
   112	
   113	That is exhaustive over `int`, with no catch-all, because the checker carries real integer
   114	intervals.
   115	
   116	**One spelling, in every position** — guard, pattern and refinement predicate. There is no `&&` and
   117	no `||`; they were removed rather than kept as synonyms. This language puts patterns in the
   118	*parameter* position, so a pattern and a guard sit on the same line in every non-trivial function.
   119	C# separates its pattern `and` from its expression `&&` deliberately, and can afford to because
   120	patterns and expressions rarely touch there; here they always do.
   121	
   122	**There is no `not`, and no `!`.** Negation is not an operator in this language. The comparisons a
   123	guard admits already come in opposite pairs — `<=` against `>`, `>=` against `<`, `!=` against `==`
   124	— so the complement of any guard the checker can read is a guard you can already write, and a `not`
   125	would compile to exactly that. Where the checker *cannot* read a predicate, negating it buys nothing
   126	either: such a clause credits nothing towards exhaustiveness, and a refinement that cannot be
   127	translated is a hard error rather than a silent widening. Which case a clause takes is the head's
   128	job, not an operator's. Typing either spelling is met by a diagnostic naming the comparison to use
   129	instead. **shipped**
