The first run exposed two parser-boundary bugs, not language-rule failures: multiline switch clauses were being analyzed only from their opening line, and signature parameter names were mistakenly treated as duplicate clause-head bindings. I’m correcting those boundaries now; this is exactly why the command-line cases are useful as end-to-end tests.

`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`
In this syntax, a clause is a structural unit that can span several lines, so line-oriented parsing is insufficient even though the declarations are line-oriented. The fix is to collect a clause through balanced braces before analyzing it, while keeping each arm’s bindings isolated.
