The broader pass exposed and corrected one implementation bug in my temporary list model: its finite abstraction was used to classify arms but not to decide final completeness, so incomplete closed lists could silently pass. That path now compares against the same abstraction at both stages. I’m at the verification gate: checking the final file for placeholders, syntax errors, executable permissions, and exact visible-case outputs.

`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`
Coverage and completeness must share one domain representation. If arm classification uses an abstract domain but final exhaustiveness falls back to the raw type, the checker can appear internally consistent while missing the very diagnostic the abstraction was built to prove.
─────────────────────────────────────────────────`
